cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  Recovering pixel-accurate forest damage maps from coarse
  aerial-survey polygons
message: If you use this software or its findings, please cite it.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Muhammad
    family-names: Saqlain
    email: weecologygsoc@gmail.com
repository-code: https://github.com/musaqlain/ads-forest-damage-segmentation
abstract: >-
  U.S. Forest Service Aerial Detection Survey (ADS) polygons mark
  roughly where forest damage is, but their boundaries do not follow
  the damage. This project tests whether the polygons can be corrected
  by rigid re-alignment (they cannot — the corrections are reshapes,
  not shifts) and instead predicts damage regions directly from 30 cm
  aerial imagery with a U-Net pretrained on TreeFinder. The main
  finding is that pixel scale, not data volume, was the bottleneck:
  cutting tiles into fixed 0.60 m/px crops instead of resizing them
  more than doubled region IoU (0.116 to 0.290) under spatially
  blocked cross-validation.
keywords:
  - remote sensing
  - semantic segmentation
  - forest health
  - deep learning
  - aerial imagery
  - weak supervision
license: MIT
date-released: '2026-08-08'
